DIFFERENCES WITH THE DIGIKAM SCRIPTS
------------------------------------

- No root, no sudo: Homebrew refuses root (digiKam's 01 script fails
  for this reason). The Homebrew installer asks for the password itself,
  once, to create /opt/homebrew.
- An existing /opt/homebrew is never removed, formulae are only added.
- 4 formulae (cmake, ninja, git, ccache) instead of about 40 (no boost,
  opencv, ffmpeg, exiv2, lensfun, imagemagick, mariadb...). With
  QT_FROM=brew, Qt as split modules (qtbase, qtsvg, qttools,
  qttranslations, qtimageformats) instead of the "qt" meta formula
  (qtwebengine, qtmultimedia...), plus sqlite and hidapi.
- With QT_FROM=aqt, sqlite and hidapi are built universal and static in
  $HOME/qet-homebrew-work/deps, once.
- KDE Frameworks: 3 modules (extra-cmake-modules, kcoreaddons,
  kwidgetsaddons) instead of 25, no patches, built statically in
  $HOME/qet-homebrew-work/kf6 and not in /opt/homebrew: no KF dylib to
  deploy or sign. Built once (a few minutes), kept for the next builds.
- No Packages .pkg installer nor package_lib.py: QET ships a
  drag-and-drop DMG, signed and notarized, as before.
- Not interactive: the questions of MacQetDeploy_arm64_cmake.sh are
  settings in config.sh.


FILES
-----

build-aux/macos/homebrew/
├── homebrew.readme           This file
├── config.sh                 Settings: target, formulae, QET branch, signing
├── common.sh                 Shared functions
├── 01-build-homebrew.sh      Homebrew (if missing), build tools, Qt 6
├── 02-build-qet.sh           sqlite, hidapi, KF6 (once), then QElectroTech
├── 03-build-installer.sh     macdeployqt, QET data, signature, DMG, notarization
├── makeall.sh                01 + 02 + 03 : everything from scratch
└── update.sh                 02 + 03 : rebuild QET and the DMG


REQUIREMENTS
------------

- A Mac with macOS 14 or newer. Apple Silicon for QT_FROM=brew; an Intel
  Mac also works with QT_FROM=aqt.
- Xcode Command Line Tools: xcode-select --install
- A normal user, admin of the Mac (for the Homebrew installer only).
- To sign: the "Developer ID Application" certificate in the login
  keychain. To notarize: a notarytool profile, created once with
    xcrun notarytool store-credentials "org.qelectrotech" \
          --apple-id <apple id> --team-id Y73WZ6WZ5X
- Internet access to: github.com (Homebrew, QET, sqlite, hidapi), the
  Homebrew bottles (ghcr.io), PyPI and the Qt mirrors (aqtinstall),
  invent.kde.org (KDE Frameworks).


CONFIGURATION (config.sh)
-------------------------

OSX_MIN_TARGET   Minimal macOS version (14.0), written in the binaries and
                 in Info.plist. 02-build-qet.sh stops if the Qt used needs
                 a newer macOS (its "minos"), instead of producing a DMG
                 that older Macs refuse with the -10825 error.
QT_FROM          aqt (default, universal) or brew (arm64 only).
QT_VERSION       Qt version installed by aqt (6.11.2).
QT_AQT_MODULES   Extra Qt modules: qtpdf (PDF page import), qtimageformats.
OSX_ARCHS        "arm64;x86_64" (universal, default) or one of them, with
                 QT_FROM=aqt. Every binary of the bundle is checked with lipo.
SQLITE_VERSION,  Versions of the universal sqlite and hidapi built with
HIDAPI_VERSION   QT_FROM=aqt.
BREW_*           Homebrew formulae to install. BREW_QT_OPTIONAL is
                 installed only if Homebrew has it (QtPdf: PDF page import).
QET_VERSION      QET branch or tag cloned in $HOME/qet-homebrew-work/build.
QET_SOURCE_DIR   Build an existing checkout as it is, instead of cloning:
                   QET_SOURCE_DIR=~/qelectrotech-source-mirror ./update.sh
QET_WITH_KF      1 = KF6 (default), 0 = QET's sources/ui/nokde replacements.
KF_VERSION       KF6 version, same as cmake/fetch_kdeaddons.cmake (v6.10.0).
                 Changing it rebuilds KF6 at the next run.
QET_SPACEMOUSE   3D mouse support with hidapi.
QET_SIGN         1 = Developer ID signature, 0 = ad hoc (local tests and
                 CI: Gatekeeper blocks it at first launch, no notarization).
QET_NOTARIZE     Notarize and staple the .app and the DMG.
QET_UPLOAD       Upload with rsync to QET_UPLOADURL:QET_UPLOADDIR.
                 These three can be given in the environment.


USAGE
-----

  cd build-aux/macos/homebrew
  ./makeall.sh            first time: Homebrew, KF6, QET, DMG
  ./update.sh             next times: QET and DMG only

Logs are in ./logs, the DMG and its SHA256 file in ./bundle.

To test the Intel part of a universal DMG on an Apple Silicon Mac:

  arch -x86_64 /Applications/qelectrotech.app/Contents/MacOS/qelectrotech


CONTINUOUS INTEGRATION
----------------------

.github/workflows/macos-dmg.yml runs these scripts on a GitHub macOS
runner, with QET_SIGN=0 (no certificate in the CI) and QET_UPLOAD=0, and
publishes the universal DMG on the nightly release. It is called by
windows-msi.yml, like the Linux AppImage. Qt, KF6, sqlite and hidapi are
cached: only QET is built on each run.

The CI DMG is only signed ad hoc: macOS blocks it at first launch. Allow
it in System Settings > Privacy & Security > "Open Anyway", or:

  xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/qelectrotech.app

Releases stay signed and notarized on a Mac with the Developer ID.


